same error here — and I can confirm it's specific to auto-compaction of long sessions on the free Zen tier, with a reproducible trigger.

What I observed: Builds (normal chat) always succeed; only auto-compaction fails with this FreeTierError, once the session grows large (~900+ messages, ~2.2–2.4 MB stored). 15 consecutive compaction failures in the same session, each immediately after a successful build. A brand-new session compacts fine → not an account-wide or server-wide outage.

Root cause (from the desktop bundle, SessionCompaction.compactAfterOverflow): the compaction request is the only one sent as a single giant user message containing the whole serialized conversation, with tools: [] and no system prompt — reusing identical x-opencode-* headers from the parent build request:

llm.stream(request({
  model, http: input.request.http,
  messages: [Message.user(summaryPrompt)],
  tools: [], generation: { maxTokens: summaryOutput }
}))
It's the only request shape that gets rejected, while builds with the same headers/identity always pass → the Zen free-tier gate keyed on the large single-message / no-tools body, not on auth or headers.

Suggestion: either accept legitimate compaction requests from the OpenCode client on the free tier, or chunk/trim the single compaction message so it stays under gateway limits. Happy to attach the full log timeline + DB stats (session size at failure onset) if useful.

If you're stuck on this and can't opt in to China hosting: opencode lets you add any OpenAI-compatible provider next to Go, so you can keep Go for the other models and point DeepSeek somewhere else.

Disclosure: I run a small inference API (inferenceapis.com), so I'm biased, but this is exactly the case it covers. DeepSeek V4 Flash (the same 0731 build), V4.1 Flash and V4 Pro run on GPUs in the United States, requests are never forwarded to DeepSeek's API, and zero data retention is on with the GPU host. It's pay per token, with no 5-hour window: V4 Flash is $0.19 in / $0.38 out / $0.04 cached per million tokens. That is more than DeepSeek's own API, and less than what Go meters the same model at during peak hours.

Add this to `opencode.json`, set `INFERENCE_API_KEY`, then pick it in `/models`:

```json
{
  "provider": {
    "inferenceapis": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "name": "Inference APIs",
      "options": { "baseURL": "https://api.inferenceapis.com/v1", "apiKey": "{env:INFERENCE_API_KEY}" },
      "models": {
        "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash": { "name": "DeepSeek V4 Flash", "limit": { "context": 1000000, "output": 32768 } },
        "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Pro": { "name": "DeepSeek V4 Pro", "limit": { "context": 1000000, "output": 32768 } }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

I tested this exact config with opencode 1.18.31 today (read a file, fix a bug, write and run tests): V4 Flash took 12 seconds and cost under a cent. I wrote up the error, the opt-in switch and all the options, including the ones that aren't mine, here: https://inferenceapis.com/reference/errors/opencode-go-model-only-available-hosted-in-china?src=gh39845

